{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R23", "R21", "U4"],
      "pins": ["R23.1", "R23.2", "R21.1", "R21.2", "U4.13"],
      "nets": ["/VSEL_EC", "/GNDISO_EC", "/V_EC", "/VISO_EC"],
      "observed": "The EC isolator secondary-side voltage-select pin is not configured like the pH channel. U4 pin 13 (VSEL) is tied through R21 (16.9 kΩ) to /V_EC and also through R23 (10 kΩ) to /GNDISO_EC, forming a resistive divider rather than a clean high select for the isolated 5 V output.",
      "expected": "The specification states that both isolated EZO channels derive their own isolated +5 V supplies (/V_pH and /V_EC). For ADM3260, VSEL should be selected high for 5 V (or otherwise unambiguously configured for 5 V), as the pH channel is pulled up to /V_pH via R20. The EC channel should not be pulled toward isolated ground with R23 if /V_EC is intended to be +5 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"68\") (name \"/VSEL_{EC}\") ... (node (ref \"R21\") (pin \"1\")) ... (node (ref \"R23\") (pin \"1\")) ... (node (ref \"U4\") (pin \"13\") (pinfunction \"VSEL\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R23\") (value \"10k\") ... )"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "Each isolated EZO channel (U3 for pH, U4 for EC) derives its own isolated +5V (/V_pH, /V_EC) and isolated ground (/GNDISO_pH, /GNDISO_EC) internally from the ADM3260's isoPower feature"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The EC isolated supply may be set to 3.3 V or an undefined intermediate voltage instead of the intended 5 V, potentially preventing the Atlas Scientific EZO-EC circuit from operating correctly and corrupting EC/TDS measurements.",
      "confidence": 0.58
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["M4"],
      "pins": ["M4.6"],
      "nets": ["+3V3"],
      "observed": "The EZO-RTD circuit M4 is powered from the +3V3 rail: M4 pin 6 (VCC) is connected to +3V3.",
      "expected": "Atlas Scientific EZO circuits, including the EZO-RTD, are normally powered from 5 V (the pH and EC channels in this design are supplied with isolated +5 V). If the RTD circuit requires 5 V, it should be connected to the board's +5V rail rather than +3V3.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"1\") (name \"+3V3\") ... (node (ref \"M4\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"VCC\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ... )"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "the EZO-RTD (M4) does not — it shares the main, non-isolated I2C bus"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "Each isolated EZO channel (U3 for pH, U4 for EC) derives its own isolated +5V (/V_pH, /V_EC)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the EZO-RTD requires a 5 V supply, water-temperature sensing through the PT-1000 probe may fail or operate outside specification.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The most plausible anomaly is the EC isolated-supply VSEL configuration: unlike the pH channel, U4's VSEL pin is pulled toward isolated ground through R23, which can prevent /V_EC from being the intended isolated +5 V. A secondary possible issue is that the EZO-RTD circuit M4 is powered from +3V3 rather than the 5 V supply normally required by EZO circuits."
}

